1 OCCU Írsk konungsdóttir, Først slave og senere elskerinde - se Laxdæla saga. Irsk prinsesse, Írsk konungsdóttir. Fylgikona Höskuldar., Irish Princess, kidnapped, sold as a slave, bought by Hoskuld Dala-Kollsson, farming at Melkorkustea
2 CONC d.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  
2 CONT
2 CONT Melkorka is the name given in Landnámabók and Laxdœla saga for the Irish mother of the Icelandic goði Ólafr Höskuldsson. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Laxdœla saga, Höskuldr purchased Melkorka, who he believed to be a mute thrall-woman, from a Rus' merchant on Brännö while on a trading expedition to Norway, and made her his concubine while away from his wife Jórunn 
2 CONC Bjarnadóttir. When Höskuldr returned home to Iceland, he took her with him. Despite Jórunn's irritation, the concubine was accepted into Höskuldr's household, though he remained faithful to Jórunn while in Iceland. The following w
2 CONC inter the concubine gave birth to a son, to whom they gave the name Ólafr after Höskuldr's uncle, Olaf Feilan, who had recently died. Landnámabók mentions that Höskuldr and Melkorka had another son, Helgi, but he does not appear i
2 CONC n Laxdœla. According to Laxdœla saga, Ólafr was a precocious child, and could speak and walk perfectly by the age of two. One day Höskuldr discovered Ólafr's mother speaking to her son; she was not, in fact, mute. When he confront
2 CONC ed her she told him that she was an Irish princess named Melkorka carried off in a viking raid, and that her father was an Irish king named "Myrkjartan" (Muirchertach). Shortly thereafter squabbling between Jórunn and Melkorka for
2 CONC ced Höskuldr to move his concubine and his son by her to a different farm, which thereafter was known as Melkorkustaðir. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 956, Ólafr, at Melkorka's urging, decided to go abroad to seek his fortune. Melkorka taught Ólafr Irish Gaelic and urged him to visit her family. Höskuldr was opposed to the expedition and would not provide trade wares, and
2 CONC  the property of Ólafr's foster-father Þórðr was mostly in immobile goods and land. In part to arrange financing for his expedition, his mother Melkorka married Þorbjörn skrjúpur ("the Feeble"), a farmer who had previously assiste
2 CONC d her in the management of Melkorkustaðir. Melkorka and Þorbjörn had a son named Lambi. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ólafr visited Ireland, where he met Melkorka's father and kinsmen. Ólafr remained with Myrkjartan for a time, and the king, according to Laxdœla saga, even offered to make Ólafr his heir. Ólafr, however, ultimately returned to Nor
2 CONC way, afraid of provoking Myrkjartan's sons. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Melkorka is featured in the 2007 young adult novel Hush: An Irish Princess' Tale by author Donna Jo Napoli. The novel depicts how she became a slave and why she decided to remain mute. The novel ends as she is being taken to Icela
2 CONC nd by Höskuldr.
2 CONT
2 CONT Melkorka was found not to be mute after she was overheard talking to her Son Olaf. When Hoskuldr confronted her she explained that she was an Irish Princess ( her father was Muirchertach mac Neill) and she was taken after a Viking
2 CONC  raid. Soon after bickering began between Hoskuldr's Wife Jorunn & Melkorka. This Forced Hoskuldr to remove his Concubine Melkorka & Olaf to farmland near by, this was called Melkorkustaðir after Melkorka.  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT #http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melkorka
2 CONT
