1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1.        ''' [https://www.geni.com/people/Duncan-M%C3%B3rmaer-of-Caithness/6000000003827374125?through=6000000003827374118 DUNGAD] [Duncan] .  Mormaer of Caithness.  m [https://www.geni.com/people/Groa-Thorsteinsd
2 CONC ottir/6000000003827374118?through=6000000000161219866 GROA], daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Thorstein-the-Red-Olafsson/6000000000161219866?through=6000000003827374118 THORSTEIN "the Red"].'''  Dungad & his wife had one c
2 CONC hild:  [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY.htm#_Toc268450047 (Cawley's Medlands)]
2 CONT
2 CONT >a)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Grelod-Duncansdatter/6000000000160986426?through=6000000003827374125  GRELAD] .  Snorre names "Grelad, a daughter of Earl Dungad of Caithness…[and of] Groa, a daughter of Thorstein Raud" as
2 CONC  mother of the five sons of Thorfin "Hausakljufer"[232].  m (941) [https://www.geni.com/people/Thorfinn-Skull-Splitter-Jarl-of-Orkney/6000000000160866853?through=6000000000160986426 THORFINN] "Hausakliffer/Skullcleaver" Jarl of Or
2 CONC kney, son of EINAR "Turf-Einar" Jarl of Orkney & his wife --- (-soon after 977).  He appears to have become Jarl of Caithness.  [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY.htm#_Toc268450047 (Cawley's Medlands)]
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mormaer of Caithness is not the same as Mormaer of Atholl:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mormaerdom_of_Caithness
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT His name is preserved in Duncansby Head (profile pic)
2 CONT Ross, David: ‘Ancient Scotland’, Geddes & Grosset, Scotland 2004.  _____________
2 CONT
2 CONT Duncan and Grelad had one daughter, Groa. She married Thorfinn Hausakljufr (the skullsplitter), Earl of Orkney. Thorfinn succeeded as Mormaer of Caithness. After this, the Mormaerdom of Caithness was in the hands of the norse Earl
2 CONC s of Orkney. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Skuli Thorfinnsson, son of Thorfinn and grandson of Duncan had the mormaership of Caithness confirmed by King Kenneth II of Alba.
