2 CONT =Ingrid Ragnvaldsdotter, From Wikipedia=
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingrid Ragnvaldsdotter of Sweden (Old Norse: Ingiríðr Rögnvaldsdóttir) (born 1100/1110- d. after 1161 AD) was born a member of the Swedish royal family, became a member of Danish royalty by marriage and later was Queen Consort of 
2 CONC Norway as the spouse of Harald IV of Norway. Married four times, Ingrid had a number of children who played a prominent role in Swedish and Norwegian history.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingrid Ragnvaldsdotter was born between 1100 and 1110. History has not recorded either the name nor background of Ingrid's mother. She was the daughter of Ragnvald Ingesson, the only known son and heir of King Inge I of Sweden. Ho
2 CONC wever, Ragnvald died early and never succeeded as king. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingrid first married Henry Skadelaar (Henrik Svendsen Skadelår), son of Svend Svendsen of Denmark, who was one of the bastard sons of King Sweyn II of Denmark. Svend Svendsen was an unsuccessful pursuer to the throne, who died whe
2 CONC n he was at a point of being elected as king. Henry Skadelaar was crippled and not considered a candidate for kingship. Instead he was a frequent schemer and plotter, generating a number of enemies. Three sons of Henry and Ingrid 
2 CONC are recorded, including King Magnus II of Sweden. Ingrid is known to have intrigued for Magnus to obtain the Swedish throne, in Ingrid's opinion her late father's rightful inheritance. She reportedly suggested that her son Magnus 
2 CONC employ the man who would kill Sverker I of Sweden.[3] Henry Skadelaar died on June 4, 1134 at the Battle of Fotevik in the Bay of Fotevik near Vellinge in Skåne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingrid soon after married King Harald IV of Norway. By him, Ingrid had a son who later became King Inge I of Norway. When King Harald was murdered in 1136, Ingrid played a prominent part in having her son, Inge, and her stepson, S
2 CONC igurd, proclaimed co-rulers, and in waging the ensuing war against the attempted usurper, Sigurd Slembe. She remained one of the most important advisors of King Inge throughout his reign. [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her third husband was Ottar Birting (Óttarr Birtingr), a prominent lendmann, but she was again widowed when he was killed in Nidaros at some point in the 1140s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between her third and fourth husband, Ingrid bore a child out of wedlock, Orm Ivarsson, by an otherwise unknown man called Ivar Sneis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingrid's fourth and last husband was Arne Ivarsson of Stårheim, (Árni Ívarsson á Stoðreimi), another prominent lendmann with whom she had four children. Arne Ivarsson was subsequently called Kongsmåg meaning in-law of the king.
2 CONT
2 CONT As an advisor to her son King Inge, Ingrid played a prominent part in many of the events of the early civil war era in Norway. On 3 February 1161, King Inge was defeated and killed, leading his men into battle against King Haakon 
2 CONC II of Norway. In the saga Heimskringla, Ingrid is last mentioned when she and her husband left Norway for exile in Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Notes on Her Name'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingrid's name can be found in a wide variety of spellings in the modern Scandinavian languages and English. Her first name, in Old Norse Ingiríðr, is rendered as Ingrid or Ingerid. Her patronym, in Old Norse Rögnvaldsdóttir, can b
2 CONC e rendered as Ragnvaldsdotter, Ragvaldsdotter or Ragnvaldsdatter.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''By Henry Sweynson (Henrik Skadelår)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Magnus II of Sweden''' (Magnus Henriksson) - who arranged the murder of Saint Eric IX of Sweden, and possibly also Sverker I of Sweden; was himself murdered; and was regarded a usurper afterwards
2 CONT
2 CONT  Jarl Ragnvald Henriksson (Ragvald Henriksson) - Riksjarl of Sweden during his brother's brief reign
2 CONT
2 CONT Burits Henriksson (also Buris/Boris; likely 1130–1167, murdered), Earl of Jutland, in 1166 married with a daughter (b. 1150) of Luitgard of Stade and Hermann II, Count of Winzenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT by Harald Gille (Haraldr Gilli)
2 CONT
2 CONT King Inge I of Norway (Ingi Haraldsson)
2 CONT
2 CONT by Ivar Sneis (Ívarr Sneis)
2 CONT
2 CONT Orm Ivarsson (Ormr konungsbróðir) (illegitimate) - prominent leader during the reign of Magnus V of Norway (Erlingsson), and after the death of Erling Skakke, next to the king in power.
2 CONT
2 CONT by Arne Ivarsson of Stårheim (Árni Ívarsson á Stoðreimi)
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip of Herdla (Philippus í Herðlu)
2 CONT
2 CONT Bishop Nicholas Arnesson (Nikolás Árnason) - co-founder of the Bagler party
2 CONT
2 CONT Inge Arnesson (Ingi Árnason)
2 CONT
2 CONT Margrete Arnesdotter (Margrét Árnadóttir), who became mother of the Bagler king Philip Simonsson
2 CONT
