1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Muirchertach mac Néill (c. 890 – 943), widely celebrated in history as Muirchertach of the Leather Cloaks (Muirchertach na Cochall Craicinn), was an exceptionally powerful 10th-century King of Ailech and the design
2 CONC ated heir (Rígdamna) to the High Kingship of Ireland. He earned his famous nickname from the heavy sheepskin leather cloaks he commissioned for his soldiers to protect them during freezing winter campaigns.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Niall Glúndub mac Áedo, the 170th High King of Ireland (d. 919).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Gormlaith ingen Flann Sinna, an iconic Irish queen and poet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife: Flann ingen Donnchaid, a princess of Mide and daughter of High King Donnchad Donn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muirchertach rose to prominence after his father was killed by the Vikings in 919. Assuming military leadership of the Northern Uí Néill, he became one of the most relentless and successful anti-Viking commanders of his era, repea
2 CONC tedly defeating the Norse forces of Dublin, Newry, and the Hebrides.
2 CONT
2 CONT His greatest military achievement occurred in the winter of 941, when he launched a legendary grand circuit of Ireland. Leading a synchronized march around the island, he took royal hostages from every provincial kingdom—including
2 CONC  the King of Dublin and the King of Munster—to completely secure his future succession to the High Kingship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before he could officially claim the High Throne, Muirchertach was killed in a fierce vanguard battle against the forces of Blácaire mac Gofrith, King of Dublin, at Ardee on 26 February 943. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His death notice in the Annals of Ulster famously laments him as "the Hector of the western world."
2 CONT
2 CONT He left behind several children, 
2 CONT
2 CONT #Domnall ua Néill (c. 915 – 980) – Highly capable military reformer and 173rd High King of Ireland.
2 CONT #Flaithbertach (d. 949) 
2 CONT #Murchadh
2 CONT #Flann
2 CONT #Dúnflaith ingen Muirchertaig (d. 999)
2 CONT
2 CONT #(Melkorka Mýrkjartansdóttir is classified as an alleged or semi-legendary daughter from the Laxdæla saga.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hugo O'Neill, heir apparent (in 1970 anyway) to the Chieftainry of
2 CONT the O'Neills of Clannaboy (b. 1939).
2 CONT 2. Jorge O'Neill, the O'Neill of Clannaboy (b. 1908).
2 CONT 3. Hugo Joseph Jorge Ever O'Neill (1874-1940).
