1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Begrepet Munkathvera er oppført i engelskspråklig Wikipedia. Der heter det:
2 CONT
2 CONT Coordinates: 65°32′44″N 18°04′58″W﻿ / ﻿65.54551°N 18.08268°W
2 CONT  Munkaþverá (transliterated as Munkathvera) was a Benedictine monastery in Eyjafjörður, Iceland. It was established around 1155 and was abolished when the country was reformed in 1550. The monastery is best known about its early A
2 CONC bbot Níkulás Bergsson and his famous publication Leiðarvísir og borgarskipan, which was basically a guidebook for pilgrims about the routes from Northern Europe to Rome and Jerusalem. The monastery once was the home of such heroes
2 CONC  of saga literature as Einar Þveræingur, Víga-Glúmur and Bergur Sokkason. It is believed to be the burial place of Sighvatur Sturluson and his sons who died in the Battle of Örlygsstaðir.
2 CONT  The current church in Munkaþverá is built in 1844. In front of the church, there is a memorial dedicated to Jón Arason, who attended the monastery and did his studies there.
