1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Annar son Óttars var Helgi; hann herjaði á Skotland og tók þar að herfangi Niðbjörgu, dóttur Bjólans konungs og Kaðlínar, dóttur Göngu-Hrólfs; hann fékk hennar; var son þeirra Ósvífur hinn spaki og Einar skálaglamm
2 CONC , er drukknaði á Einarsskeri í Selasundi, og kom skjöldur hans í Skjaldey, en feldur á Feldarhólm. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Just að thought of Anna Petursdottir concerning the name "Bjólan". It has come in to my mind whether the name "Bjólan" would be correctly written as "Beolan" or "O´Beolan": https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fearchar,_Earl_of_Ross . Th
2 CONC e article in Wikipedia refers to a much younger source and people, but then the family name "Beolan" could also be ancient. It is stated on page 223 in the book "The Highlanders of Scotland; their origin, History and Antiquities (
2 CONC author William F. Skeene)" that the oldest known sources of the name "Beolan" dates probably from the year 1162. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Smá hugleiðing mín um nafnið „Bjólan“! Mér hefur dottið í hug hvort að ekki sé rétt að nafnð „Bjólan“ myndi ekki vera rétt skrifað sem „Beolan“ eða „O‘ Beolan“:  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fearchar,_Earl_of_Ross . Greinin í Wik
2 CONC ipedia vísar í miklu yngri heimildir en „Bjólan“ hér að ofan er en nafnið „Beolan“ gæti verið fornt ættarnafn fyrir því.  Einnig er skráð í bókinni "The Highlanders of Scotland; their origin, History and Antiquities (author Willia
2 CONC m F. Skeene)" á bls. 223 að elstu þekktu heimildir um nafnið „Beolan“ sé frá því um 1162 [sign: Anna Petursdottir].
2 CONT
2 CONT Taken from Wikipedia (https://is.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6ngu-Hr%C3%B3lfur ): According to the medieval Irish text An Banshenchas and Icelandic sources, another daughter, Cadlinar (Kaðlín; Kathleen) was born in Scotland (probably
2 CONC  to a Scots mother) and married an Irish prince named Beollán mac Ciarmaic, later King of South Brega (Lagore). A daughter of Cadlinar and Beollán named Nithbeorg was abducted by an Icelandic Viking named Helgi Ottarsson,[25][26] 
2 CONC and became the mother of the poet Einarr Helgason and grandmother of Guðrún Ósvífrsdóttir (protagonist of the Laxdœla saga). 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT King Bjolan (Scots Beolan) would be a tribal king of a clan existing in parts of north east Ireland and Wester Ross Scotland...King of Wester Ross (North Argyll), Scotland and King of South Lagore (Brega), Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The earlier Pictish abbots of Applecross were probably "co-arbs" (bloodrelated successors) of St. Mael Rubha in the Pictish mode of matrilineal descent. Since Applecross, as a Celtic tribal abbey, was continuously active througho
2 CONC ut (t)he Viking period, we can infer a similar continuity of co-arbial succession among its abbots. Therefore the O’Beolains, despite a patrilineal connection to Helgi Bjolan (which would have served them well at the time), repres
2 CONC ent continuity with the old line. Surnames in the earlier "0" form came into use in Ireland in the tenth century..." 
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Helgi, the son of Ketill Flatnefr, nine-century Norse ruler of the Hebrides, was himself known by the Gaelic nickname of "Bjolan" (Beolain), nicknames being the usual second element in Norse personal names. Nor should we be su
2 CONC rprised to find that Helgi Bjolan’s relatives brought Christianity to Iceland in the ninth century. The very survival of Applecross as an abbey on the coast of Viking Ross points to fortuitous Norse patronage. Helgi Bjolan is undo
2 CONC ubtedly the namesake of the O’Beolains, since his father was ruler of the Hebrides (Applecross faces these) and his nephew was King of Ross. Beolain is certainly not a typical Gaelic name... The main line of the co-arbial abbots o
2 CONC f Applecross later became vested in the Earldom of Ross under the Normanized Kings..."
2 CONT
2 CONT Comment: The source also says in effect (p. 36), and in context of Helgi Bjolan and the O'Beolans, that Viking royalty often married Celtic princesses as a way to enhance their local control. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (http://www.electricscotland.com/webclans/cairney/37.htm).
2 CONT
2 CONT Beollan was the Irish prince Beollan son of Ciarmac, the king of Lagore ... Nithbeorg had a sister named Deichter who is remembered in the Banshenchas, where their father is called the "king of south Brega, of the treacherous Viki
2 CONC ngs."
2 CONT
