1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Halvdan Sigurdsson''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Father: [http://www.geni.com/people/Sigurd-Halvdansson-%C2%ABsow%C2%BB-Syr/6000000001200208116 Sigurd Halvdansson «sow» Syr], mother [http://www.geni.com/people/%C3%85sta-Astrid-Gudbrandsdotter/6000000001199952528  Åsta (Astrid)
2 CONC  Gudbrandsdotter].
2 CONT
2 CONT Halvdan av Stein (999 -) gift Ukjent (okänd), deras barn: Berglijot Halvdansdr (1016 -) gift Finn Arnasson til Austrått. (Ursprunget för de norska kungariket, sid 7)
2 CONT
2 CONT Det berättas att när kung Olav var på besök till sin mor Asta tog hon ut sina barn, och visade dem till honom. Kungen tog hans bror Guthorm å ena knäet, och hans bror Halfdan på den andra. Kungen tittade på Guthorm, gjorde ett sne
2 CONC tt ansikte och låtsades vara arg på dem: där pojkarna var rädda. Sedan Asta förde hennes yngsta son, som heter Harald, som var tre år gammal, till honom. (Heimskringla, Olov den Heliges saga II)
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Halvdan av Stein (999 -) married Ukjent (unknown), deras barn: Berglijot Halvdansdr (1016 -) married Finn Arnasson til Austrått. (The Origin of the Norwegian Kingdom, sid 7)
2 CONT
2 CONT It is told that when King Olaf was on his visit to his mother Asta, she brought out her children, and showed them to him. The king took his brother Guthorm on the one knee, and his brother Halfdan on the other. The king looked at 
2 CONC Guthorm, made a wry face, and pretended to be angry at them: at which the boys were afraid. Then Asta brought her youngest son, called Harald, who was three years old, to him. (Heimskringla, Olov den Heliges saga II)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.espell.se/saga/p1e0eebef.html
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He was half-brother of King Óláfr the Saint and full-brother of King Haraldr who died in 1066 in a battle in England. When King Óláfr asked him what he would like most to have, he said "cows." When asked how many he replied "So ma
2 CONC ny that when they were watered they would stand thickly around the whole pond."
