1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the wife of https://www.geni.com/people/Fergal-O-Ruarc/6000000008630638078?through=6000000008640455501
2 CONT Not the mother of https://www.geni.com/people/NN-U%C3%AD-Conchobair/6000000008482169634?through=6000000008640455501
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Please note that this profile connects Mór ingen Taidhg an Tuir to Domnall ua Néill (High King of Ireland, d. 980), rather than to Fergal O'Ruairc (King of Connacht, d. 966/967).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historical Evidence:
2 CONT
2 CONT #The Royal Title: Upon her death in 991, the primary chronicles explicitly record her ultimate title. The Chronicon Scotorum (Annal 991/992) states: "Mór daughter of Tadc son of Cathal son of Conchobor, queen of Ireland, dies." Th
2 CONC e Annals of the Four Masters (Annal 991) likewise record her death as "Mór, daughter of Tadhg of the Tower, son of Cathal, Queen of Ireland, died."
2 CONT
2 CONT #Chronological Alignment: The singular title of "Queen of Ireland" (Ard-Ríoghan Érenn) reflects her marriage to the supreme High King of the era. Domnall ua Néill held the High Kingship of Ireland from 956 until his death in 980—t
2 CONC he exact window of Mór's adult life and prominence.
2 CONT
2 CONT #The O'Ruairc Conflation: Fergal O'Ruairc was a powerful contemporary King of Connacht, but he was never recognized as the undisputed High King of Ireland, and he predeceased Domnall ua Néill by nearly fourteen years (d. 966/967).
2 CONC  Merging Mór to O'Ruairc stems from a modern misunderstanding of the dynamic Connacht regional branches and creates severe chronological conflicts.
2 CONT
2 CONT
