2 CONT Original individual @P2442106131@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@) merged with @P2308132308@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@)
1 NOTE [large-G675.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Macbeth siezed the throne of Scotland in 1040 after defeating and killingDuncan I near elgin. He based his claim to the crown on his wife's royaldescent (Duncan's former wife Sybal). Malcolm III, son of Duncan I, andEarl Siward of
2 CONC  Northumberland defeated Macbeth at Dunsinane in 1054, butthey did not dethrone him. Three years later, Malcolm III killed Macbethat Lumphanan. Macbeth's stepson Lulach reigned for a few months, and thenMalcolm III succeeded him a
2 CONC s king.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Shakespeare based his play, Macbeth, one of his greatesttragedies, upon a distorted version of these events which he found inRaphael Holinshed's 'Chronicle of Scottish History.' The only kernel ofhistorical truth in the pl
2 CONC ay is Duncan's death at the hand of Macbeth.From this fact, Shakespeare drew his portrait of ambition leading to aviolent and tragic end.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: 'The World Book Encyclopedia', 1968, p M4. 'Ancestrial Roots ofSixty Colonists Who Came to New England between 1623 and 1650', 1969,Frederick Lewis Weis, p 111.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: 'Ancestrial Roots of Sixty Colonists Who Came to New Englandbetween 1623 and 1650', 1969, Frederick Lewis Weis, p 111. 'Royalty forCommoners', Roderick W. Stuart, 1993, p 2.
2 CONT
2 CONT Acceded 1057-1093.[large-G675.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Macbeth siezed the throne of Scotland in 1040 after defeating and killingDuncan I near elgin. He based his claim to the crown on his wife's royaldescent (Duncan's former wife Sybal). Malcolm III, son of Duncan I, andEarl Siward of
2 CONC  Northumberland defeated Macbeth at Dunsinane in 1054, butthey did not dethrone him. Three years later, Malcolm III killed Macbethat Lumphanan. Macbeth's stepson Lulach reigned for a few months, and thenMalcolm III succeeded him a
2 CONC s king.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Shakespeare based his play, Macbeth, one of his greatesttragedies, upon a distorted version of these events which he found inRaphael Holinshed's 'Chronicle of Scottish History.' The only kernel ofhistorical truth in the pl
2 CONC ay is Duncan's death at the hand of Macbeth.From this fact, Shakespeare drew his portrait of ambition leading to aviolent and tragic end.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: 'The World Book Encyclopedia', 1968, p M4. 'Ancestrial Roots ofSixty Colonists Who Came to New England between 1623 and 1650', 1969,Frederick Lewis Weis, p 111.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: 'Ancestrial Roots of Sixty Colonists Who Came to New Englandbetween 1623 and 1650', 1969, Frederick Lewis Weis, p 111. 'Royalty forCommoners', Roderick W. Stuart, 1993, p 2.
1 NOTE Criança quando o pai foi assassinado por Macbeth (Mac Bethad mac Findlaích), passou a infância na Nortúmbria refugiado com o tio, o conde Siward, que o estabeleceu em 1054 na Cúmbria e em Lothian, tendo então adotado costumes angl
2 CONC o-saxões. Permaneceu 15 anos na corte saxônica, no reinado de Eduardo. Até 1058 pode observar o estilo de governo dos reis e como Eduardo era altamente impopular com os aristocratas saxões, pode conhecer também os problemas.
2 CONT Por vingança, destruiu o castelo de Inverness e levantou outro perto, na colina do Castelo ou Castle Hill. Invadiu a Inglaterra cinco vezes entre 1061 e 1093.
1 NOTE "DUNKELD"; KNOWN AS "CAENMOR"(GREAT HEAD)"MACDUNCAN"; KING OF SCOTS 1058-1093;
2 CONT REGAINED PROVINCES FROM THORFINN, KILLING HIM AT LUMPHANAN; SLAIN WHILE
2 CONT BESIEGING ALNWICK CASTLE
1 NOTE Malcom III Canmore van Schotland, geb. 1031, ovl. 13.11.1093 (vermoord), begraven in Abdij van Dunfermline, ref. nr. 23.06.2003 ES II-78 /-89, HLp592.6,55                         Koning der Schotten 1058. Trouwt (1) ca. 1059 Ingeb
2 CONC org van Halland, d.v. Jarl Finn Arnesson en Berglioth van Noorwegen, weduwe van Thorfin II Earl of Caithness.                         Hij trouwde met Margareta de Heilige van Engeland, getrouwd 1069/70.
1 NOTE Founder of the dynasty that consolidated royal power in the Scottish kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of King Duncan I (reigned 1034-40), Malcolm lived in exile in England during part of the reign of his father's murderer, Macbeth (reigned 1040-57). Malcolm killed Macbeth in battle in 1057 and then ascended the throne. Aft
2 CONC er the conquest of England by William the Conqueror, in 1066, Malcolm gave refuge to the Anglo-Saxon prince Edgar the Aetheling and his sisters, one of whom, Margaret (later St. Margaret), became his second wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Malcolm acknowledged the overlordship of William in 1072 but nevertheless soon violated his feudal obligations and made five raids into England. During the last of these invasions he was killed by the forces of King William II Ruf
2 CONC us (reigned 1087-1100). Except for a brief interval after Malcolm's death, the Scottish throne remained in his family until the death of Queen Margaret, the Maid of Norway, in 1290. Of Malcolm's six sons by Margaret, three succeed
2 CONC ed to the throne: Edgar (reigned 1097-1107), Alexander I (1107-24), and David I (1124-53).
1 NOTE !SOURCES:
2 CONT     1. Scots Peerage, Scot 2b, v. 1, p. 1-3
2 CONT     2. Keiser und Koenig Hist., Gen. Hist. 25, pt. 1, p. 96-97
