2 CONT >>3.         [http://www.geni.com/people/Thorfinn-I-Hausakljufer-Einarsson/6000000000160866853 THORFINN] "Hausakliffer/Skullcleaver" ([910/20]-[soon after 977], bur Hoxa, North Ronaldsway).  Orkneyinga Saga names “[http://www.geni
2 CONC .com/people/Arnkell/6000000001169221716   Arnkel] …[http://www.geni.com/people/Erlend/6000000001169221723?through=6000000001045369865  Erlend] and…[http://www.geni.com/people/Thorfinn-I-Hausakljufer-Einarsson/6000000000160866853  
2 CONC Thorfinn] Skull-Splitter”  as the three sons of Einar[820].  Snorre names "Thorfin Hausakljufer, a son of Torfeinar" when recording that he was Jarl in Orkney when the islands were invaded by the sons of King [http://www.geni.com/
2 CONC people/Eirik-I-Haraldsson-%C2%ABBlood-axe%C2%BB-Blod%C3%B8ks-c-885/6000000003094359287  Eirik "Blodøks"] [821].  Joint Jarl of Orkney with his brothers.  Sole Jarl of Orkney 954, and 955-976.  Orkneyinga Saga records that “Earl Th
2 CONC orfinn Skull-Splitter…died in his bed” and was “laid in a burial mound at Hoxa in North Ronaldsway”[822].  The Complete Peerage estimates Thorfinn´s death to “soon after 977” which appears broadly consistent with the events in whi
2 CONC ch his children are reported as involved[823].  
2 CONT
2 CONT >>m ([941]) [http://www.geni.com/people/Grelod-Dungadsdotter/6000000000160986426?through=6000000002855667029  GRELAD] of Caithness, daughter of [http://www.geni.com/people/Donnchadh-mac-Donnchad/6000000003827374125?through=6000000
2 CONC 000160986426   DUNGAD] [Duncan] Mormaer of Caithness & his wife [http://www.geni.com/people/Gr%C3%B3a-Thorsteinsdottir/6000000003827374118?through=6000000003827374125  Groa].  Snorre names "Grelad, a daughter of Earl Dungad of Cai
2 CONC thness…[and of] Groa, a daughter of [http://www.geni.com/people/%C3%9Eorsteinn-rau%C3%B0i-%C3%93lafsson/6000000000161219866?through=6000000003827374118  Thorstein Raud"] as mother of the five sons of Thorfin "Hausakljufer"[824].] 
2 CONC
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT "He was called Torfeinar, because he cut peat for fuel, there being no firewood, as in Orkney there are no woods. He afterwards was earl over the islands, and was a mighty man. He was ugly, and blind of an eye, yet very sharp-sigh
2 CONC ted withal." (from Harald Hårfagre's saga, see link below)
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT "Han ble kalt Torv-Einar, fordi han lot skjære torv og brukte den til ved, for det var ingen skog på Orknøyene. Siden ble Einar jarl på øyene, og han var en mektig mann; han var stygg og enøyd; men han så likevel skarpere enn mang
2 CONC e andre."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnhild "Hild" Nefja was not the mother of Torf-Einar, he was the illegitimate son of Ragnvald, no records exist of his mother. In the Orkney saga, the brothers Einar, Hallad (Haddad) and Ivar are referred to as "frillesønner", s
2 CONC ons of a frille (=literally mistress, or partner - "concubine" is not a good term for Nordic countries). Whether Ragnvald's four illegitimate sons Einar, Hrollaug, Hallad and Ivar had the same mother is not known, but highly unlik
2 CONC ely.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT "Ragnvald Mørejarl var den kjæreste venn kong Harald hadde, og kongen satte ham høyt. Ragnvald jarl var gift med Hild, datter til Rolv Nevja; deres sønner var Rolv og Tore. Ragnvald jarl hadde noen frillesønner også; en het Hallad
2 CONC ; en annen Einar og en tredje Rollaug; de var voksne da de ektefødte brødrene deres var barn ennå. Rolv var en stor viking; han var så svær av vekst at ingen hest kunne bære ham, derfor gikk han til fots overalt. Han ble kalt Gang
2 CONC e-Rolv. Han herjet ofte i austerveg." (Harald Hårfagres saga)
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From Harald Hårfagres (Fairhair) saga:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Earl Ragnvald was King Harald's dearest friend, and the king had the greatest regard for him. He was married to Hild, a daughter of Rolf Nefia, and their sons were Rolf and Thorer. Earl Ragnvald had also three sons by concubines,
2 CONC  -- the one called Hallad, the second Einar, the third Hrollaug; and all three were grown men when their brothers born in marriage were still children Rolf became a great viking, and was of so stout a growth that no horse could ca
2 CONC rry him, and wheresoever he went he must go on foot; and therefore he was called Rolf Ganger. He plundered much in the East sea.
2 CONT
2 CONT [...]
2 CONT
2 CONT When Earl Ragnvald in More heard of the death of his brother Earl Sigurd, and that the vikings were in possession of the country, he sent his son Hallad westward, who took the title of earl to begin with, and had many men-at-arms 
2 CONC with him. When he arrived at the Orkney Islands, he established himself in the country; but both in harvest, winter, and spring, the vikings cruised about the isles plundering the headlands, and committing depredations on the coas
2 CONC t. Then Earl Hallad grew tired of the business, resigned his earldom, took up again his rights as an allodial owner, and afterwards returned eastward into Norway. When Earl Ragnvald heard of this he was ill pleased with Hallad, an
2 CONC d said his son were very unlike their ancestors. Then said Einar, "I have enjoyed but little honour among you, and have little affection here to lose: now if you will give me force enough, I will go west to the islands, and promis
2 CONC e you what at any rate will please you -- that you shall never see me again." Earl Ragnvald replied, that he would be glad if he never came back; "For there is little hope," said he, "that thou will ever be an honour to thy friend
2 CONC s, as all thy kin on thy mother's side are born slaves." Earl Ragnvald gave Einar a vessel completely equipped, and he sailed with it into the West sea in harvest. When he came to the Orkney Isles, two vikings, Thorer Treskeg and 
2 CONC Kalf Skurfa, were in his way with two vessels. He attacked them instantly, gained the battle, and slew the two vikings. Then this was sung: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "Then gave he Treskeg to the trolls,
2 CONT
2 CONT Torfeinar slew Skurfa."
2 CONT
2 CONT [...]
2 CONT
2 CONT Halfdan Haleg came very unexpectedly to Orkney, and Earl Einar immediately fled; but came back soon after about harvest time, unnoticed by Halfdan. They met and after a short battle Halfdan fled the same night. Einar and his men l
2 CONC ay all night without tents, and when it was light in the morning they searched the whole island and killed every man they could lay hold of. Then Einar said "What is that I see upon the isle of Rinansey? Is it a man or a bird? Som
2 CONC etimes it raises itself up, and sometimes lies down again." They went to it, and found it was Halfdan Haleg, and took him prisoner.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Einar sang the following song the evening before he went into this battle: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "Where is the spear of Hrollaug? where
2 CONT
2 CONT Is stout Rolf Ganger's bloody spear!
2 CONT
2 CONT I see them not; yet never fear,
2 CONT
2 CONT For Einar will not vengeance spare
2 CONT
2 CONT Against his father's murderers, though
2 CONT
2 CONT Hrollaug and Rolf are somewhat slow,
2 CONT
2 CONT And silent Thorer sits add dreams
2 CONT
2 CONT At home, beside the mead-bowl's streams."
2 CONT
2 CONT Thereafter Earl Einar went up to Halfdan, and cut a spread eagle upon his back, by striking his sword through his back into his belly, dividing his ribs from the backbone down to his loins, and tearing out his lungs; and so Halfda
2 CONC n was killed. Einar then sang: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "For Ragnvald's death my sword is red:
2 CONT
2 CONT Of vengeance it cannot be said
2 CONT
2 CONT That Einar's share is left unsped.
2 CONT
2 CONT So now, brave boys, let's raise a mound, --
2 CONT
2 CONT Heap stones and gravel on the ground
2 CONT
2 CONT O'er Halfdan's corpse: this is the way
2 CONT
2 CONT We Norsemen our scat duties pay."
2 CONT
2 CONT Then Earl Einar took possession of the Orkney Isles as before. Now when these tidings came to Norway, Halfdan's brothers took it much to heart, and thought that his death demanded vengeance; and many were of the same opinion. When
2 CONC  Einar heard this, he sang: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "Many a stout udal-man, I know,
2 CONT
2 CONT Has cause to wish my head laid low;
2 CONT
2 CONT And many an angry udal knife
2 CONT
2 CONT Would gladly drink of Eina's life.
2 CONT
2 CONT But ere they lay Earl Einar low, --
2 CONT
2 CONT Ere this stout heart betrays its cause,
2 CONT
2 CONT Full many a heart will writhe, we know,
2 CONT
2 CONT In the wolf's fangs, or eagle's claws."
2 CONT
2 CONT [...]
2 CONT
2 CONT King Harald now ordered a levy, and gathered a great force, with which he proceeded westward to Orkney; and when Earl Einar heard that King Harald was come, he fled over to Caithness. He made the following verses on this occasion:
2 CONC  --
2 CONT
2 CONT "Many a bearded man must roam,
2 CONT
2 CONT An exile from his house and home,
2 CONT
2 CONT For cow or horse; but Halfdan's gore
2 CONT
2 CONT Is red on Rinansey's wild shore.
2 CONT
2 CONT A nobler deed -- on Harald's shield
2 CONT
2 CONT The arm of one who ne'er will yield
2 CONT
2 CONT Has left a scar. Let peasants dread
2 CONT
2 CONT The vengeance of the Norsemen's head:
2 CONT
2 CONT I reck not of his wrath, but sing,
2 CONT
2 CONT `Do thy worst! -- I defy thee, king! --'"
2 CONT
2 CONT Men and messages, however, passed between the king and the earl, and at last it came to a conference; and when they met the earl submitted the case altogether to the king's decision, and the king condemned the earl Einar and the O
2 CONC rkney people to pay a fine of sixty marks of gold. As the bondes thought this was too heavy for them to pay, the earl offered to pay the whole if they would surrender their udal lands to him. This they all agreed to do: the poor b
2 CONC ecause they had but little pieces of land; the rich because they could redeem their udal rights again when they liked. Thus the earl paid the whole fine to the king, who returned in harvest to Norway. The earls for a long time aft
2 CONC erwards possessed all the udal lands in Orkney, until Sigurd son of Hlodver gave back the udal rights."
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Full text with Norwegian, English and Icelandic versions: 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://lind.no/nor/index.asp?lang=&emne=asatru&vis=s_e_harald_harfagre#134
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Han ble kalt Torv-Einar. Han var frillesønn.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Turf-Einar Ragnvaldsson, Earl of Orkney (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #104785
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=28 Oct 2002
2 CONT
2 CONT      Turf-Einar Ragnvaldsson, Earl of Orkney is the son of Ragnvald I 'the Wise' Eysteinsson, Earl of More and Groa (?). (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT      Turf-Einar Ragnvaldsson, Earl of Orkney was also known as Earl of Orkney. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p10479.htm#i104785
2 CONT
2 CONT På Orknøyene ble han kalt for Torv-Einar for han lot skjære torv som brensel for det vokste ikke trær der. Han ble jarl på Orknøyene og en mektig mann, enøyd og stygg å se på.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT His name was Einar, but was known as Torf-Einer because he introduced "turf" (peat) as a fuel to the islanders. After his brother, Hallad, failed to control the Danish pirates, he asked his father to let him try. He not only freed
2 CONC  the Orkneys from Danish pirates but also fought Halfdan Haaleg (a son of Harald Hårfagre who had killed Einar's father, Ragnvald). Halfdan had invaded the Orkneys but Einar prevailed. In an old viking rite, he carved an eagle on 
2 CONC the back of Haldan and sacrificed him to Odin. Harald Hårfagre, came and demanded compensation for Halfdan's death but allowed him to remain ruler of the Orkneys. Although Einar is described as ugly, he is remembered as a strong r
2 CONC uler.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.mathematical.com/rognvaldssoneinar852.html
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.ourfamilyhistories.org/getperson.php?personID=I4154&tree=00
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torf-Einarr
2 CONT
2 CONT Einarr Rögnvaldarson, Torf-Einarr or Turf-Einar (fl. early 890s–c. 910) was one of the Norse Earls of Orkney. His rise to power is related in sagas which apparently draw on verses of Einarr's own composition for inspiration. After
2 CONC  battling for control of the Northern Isles of Scotland, Einarr founded a dynasty which retained control of the islands for centuries after his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Norse Heimskringla and Orkneyinga sagas, Rögnvald had little regard for his youngest son Einarr because Einarr's mother was a slave. The sagas record that Rögnvald agreed to provide Einarr with a ship and crew in 
2 CONC the hope that he would sail away and never return. Einarr sailed to the Scottish islands, where he defeated two Danish warlords, Þórir Tréskegg (Thorir Treebeard) and Kálf Skurfa (Kalf the Scurvy), who had taken residence there, a
2 CONC nd established himself as earl.[3] It is unclear whether the account in the sagas of Einarr's conquest is accurate. Though the Historia Norvegiæ, written at the same time as the sagas but from a different source, confirms that Rög
2 CONC nvald's family conquered the islands, it gives few details. The scene in the sagas where Einarr's father scorns him is a literary device which often figures in Old Norse literature. Much of Einarr's story in the sagas appears to b
2 CONC e derived from five skaldic verses attributed to Einarr himself.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Apart from the five verses recorded in the sagas, no other examples of Torf-Einarr's poetry are known to survive, though they appear to be part of a larger body of work.[9] A couplet that commemorates Einarr's defeat of the two pi
2 CONC rate Vikings, Þórir Tréskegg (Thorir Treebeard) and Kálf Skurfa (Kalf the Scurvy),
2 CONT
2 CONT     Hann gaf Tréskegg trollum,
2 CONT     Torf-Einarr drap Skurfu.
2 CONT     He gave Treebeard to the trolls,
2 CONT     Torf-Einarr slew Scurvy.
2 CONT
2 CONT has a matching metre and alliterative similarities to the attributed verses.[9] Einarr must have had some fame as a poet, as his name is used in the Háttatal, an examination of Old Norse poetry written in the thirteenth-century, t
2 CONC o refer to a specific type of metre, Torf-Einarsháttr.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT The remainder of Einarr's long reign was apparently unchallenged, and he died in his bed of a sickness, leaving three sons, Arnkel, Erlend and Thorfinn. The sagas describe Einarr as tall, ugly and blind in one eye, but sharp-sight
2 CONC ed nonetheless.[10] Despite these apparent disabilities, as well as his low-born mother, Einarr established a dynasty which ruled the Orkney Islands until 1470.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ari Þorgilsson quotes a short section from the now lost Torf-Einar’s Saga in the Landnámabók. It begins:
2 CONT
2 CONT     '''Earl Turf-Einar (of Orkney) had a daughter in his youth, she was called Thordis. Earl Rognvald brought her up and gave her in marriage to Thorgeir Klaufi, their son was Einar, he went to Orkney to see his kinsmen; they woul
2 CONC d not own him for a kinsman; then Einar bought a ship in partnership with two brothers, Vestman and Vemund, and they went to Iceland.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT and goes on to make brief reference to young Einar's travels there. It also lists his two sons, Eyjolf and Ljot, and some details about them and their descendents.[11][1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The sagas incorrectly claim that the Earl of Orkney was called "Turf-Einarr" because he introduced the practice of burning turf or peat to the islands since wood was so scarce. The real reason for the nickname is unknown.[2] While
2 CONC  depletion of woodland could have caused a cultural shift from burning timber to peat, potentially the name arose because the sequestration of the common or allodial rights of the islanders by Einarr forced them away from coppicin
2 CONC g towards cutting turves.[12]
