1 NOTE {geni:about_me} = Ulfhild Sunesdotter=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Sune Sverkersson Sik
2 CONT * Sune Sverkersson Sik Family Geneology Chart from Project Runeberg - http://runeberg.org/antiqtid/21/0438.html
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: '''Folke Birgersson (Folkungaätten),''' born 1164 in Kimstad, Norrköping, died 1210-07-17 in Gestilren, (of Dödad in the battle of Gestilren). Earl, Knight, lawman in Östergötland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''SUNE Folkason''' (-1247).  Jarl in Sweden.  m HELENA Sverkersdatter of Sweden, daughter of SVERKER II Karlsson "den yngre" King of Sweden & his first wife Bengte Ebbesdatter [Galen].  A charter dated 1237 refers to the marria
2 CONC ge of “S. Fulconis ducis filius” and “E. Suerchonis Regis filia” after her abduction from Vreta convent
2 CONT * '''Ulf Holmgersson (Ama)''' Knight before 1276, Riksråd 1288 , died after 1290. Married: (1) Ulf Holmgersson is mentioned no earlier than 1276 and is then called a knight. He is mentioned as a councilor in 1288 and still alive i
2 CONC n 1290. He was first married to Cecilia Haraldsdotter, daughter of Harald Gudmundsson ( winged lily ). They had four sons. Married: (2) In 1284, Ulf Holmgersson received a papal permit to marry his bride Helena Siggesdotter, with 
2 CONC her he had three daughters and a son. (See: SDHK No: 1266)
2 CONT * '''BENGTA] Folkadotter'''  married JOHAN Ängel
2 CONT * '''NN Folkadotter''' married RÖRIK 
2 CONT * '''NN Folkadotter''' married ULF Karlsson "Fase" [Folkunge] Jarl in Sweden (-[1247/48]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT Ulfhild Sunesdotter was the daughter of Suno Syk (also Simon Sik and Suno Sik). Suno is considered to have been a Swedish nobleman or prince in the 12th century, son of King Sverker the Elder. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sune Sik's tomb monument in Vreta monastery church with stone from the 16th century. Sune Sik, also Simon Sik and Suno Syk (actually an older Suno Syk), is considered to have been a Swedish nobleman or prince in the 12th century, 
2 CONC son of King Sverker the Elder. [1] It is unknown if the mother was one of Sverker's two queens or another woman. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The information that he must have been the son of Sverker d.ä. comes from a medieval genealogy that is only preserved in transcript by Olaus Petri and Johannes Bureus. Olaus Petri continues the genealogy by claiming that Suno Syk 
2 CONC must have been the father of Ingrid Ylva, a task that is missing from Bureus. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Suno Syk as the king's son has been disputed until our time. Some authors have questioned the information in their works, [2] and some experts have also strongly questioned his existence, claiming that the tradition of him arose a
2 CONC round another Sune Sik who appears in the sources as a donor to Vreta monastery in the late 13th century. [3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the 16th century, a tombstone was erected in Vreta monastery church over a Sune Sik by Rasmus Ludvigsson. The stone has a Latin inscription that Markus Lindberg at Östergötland County Museum believes must date from the Midd
2 CONC le Ages and be a copy of an older stone from around 1300. The diploma that mentions the donor Sune Sik was added at this time, which is why Lindberg believes that the contemporary original stone is not reasonable may have been mad
2 CONC e in confusion by the two persons of the same name and thus may not have been made for the younger Sune Sik (since he in all probability then still lived). Lindberg consequently believes, in line with the findings of Nils Ahnlund,
2 CONC  that there are strong reasons for the later missing 14th century source (early reported by Johannes Bureus) and the original stone, which Rasmus Ludvigsson used as a basis for his monument, 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the 18th century theologian and academic Magnus Boræn, Sune's nephews Alf and Burislev Johansson were buried next to his dust in Vreta. According to the same Boræn, Sune was Duke of Östergötland, which according to Ad
2 CONC olf Schück does not need to be interpreted as becoming an earl of Sweden, but was such a territorial earl that during the early Middle Ages in Sweden more generally equated with the Latin title dux. 
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources:=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Ahnlund, Nils in Historisk tidskrift 1945 pp. 332-351 
2 CONT * Wikipedia
2 CONT * Kingship and State Formation in Sweden: 1130 - 1290, By Philip Line, page 504  - https://books.google.com/books?id=du4gbrqq-xQC&pg=PA693&lpg=PA693&dq=Ulfhild+Sunesdotter&source=bl&ots=CgL2nMOlz8&sig=ACfU3U0HIY3ZLtUXlHBCPIjZ57OWk
2 CONC kjSow&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiRp6msz6PuAhWgHDQIHUD6AFU4FBDoATADegQICRAB#v=onepage&q=Ulfhild%20Sunesdotter&f=false
2 CONT * En Släktforskningrapport För JOHAN PETERSSON STIERNSPETZ - https://www.stiernspetz.org/stiernspetz%20family/rapport.pdf
