0 @N6000000003635727535@ NOTE [4701] "Bloodline ... ", p 427-31, 8th Jarl of Orkney 950-963
0 @N6000000003949530995@ NOTE Arnkel, Erlend I and Thorfinn I on the death of their father Jarl Turf-Einar succeeded as co-Jarls of Orkney. Arnkel and Erlend were old enough to be taken into western piracy by King Eric Bloodaxe 939, and Thorfinn’s son Arnfinn 
1 CONC was of an age to marry a woman of perhaps twenty-seven in 954 or 955.
1 CONT Thorfinn I Hausakliffer (Skull-cleaver) became sole Jarl of Orkney in 954 and again 955 until 976, when Gunnhild and her surviving sons Ragnfred and Gudred came to Orkney with their adherents after the defeat and death of King Har
1 CONC ald II Greyfell at Limfiord. They departed again in 977 and Jarl Thorfinn seems to have died soon, afterwards. He m., 941, Grelod, dau. of Dungad or Duncan, Mormaer of Caithness, by Groa, dau. of Thorstein the Red (e), and had iss
1 CONC ue, Arnfinn, Havard, Hlodve, Lint and Skuli. Although Heimskringla in one place suggests that the brothers were "Jarls there" simultaneously, the Orkneyinga Saga, and Heimskringla in another place make it clear that four of them r
1 CONC uled in succession each as sole Jarl. [Complete Peerage, X:Appendix A:6]
1 CONT Note: By the statement concerning Arnfinn's marrying a 27 year old in 954/955 (she was born c927, which makes Arnfinn probably born well before 941), CP seems to put serious doubt as to the marriage date of "941" for Thorfinn & Gr
1 CONC elod.
1 CONT Curt Hofemann, curt_hofemann AT yahoo.com, added the following speculations concerning the marriage/birth dates:  Pure speculation on my part, but maybe there was a politically arranged marriage agreement made when Arnfinn was ver
1 CONC y young & that he wasn’t of marriageable age (13) until 954/5. 955 - 13 = DOB of 942 which would fit perfectly for his parents to have married in 941. Note also that 954 was when Ragnhild’s father, Eric Bloodaxe, was killed with A
1 CONC rnfinn’s 2 uncles & Eric’s wife & family fled back to Orkney. Perhaps it was a win-win for both families. Ranghild (now without land/title) married the future Jarl of Orkney & Caithness & Arnfinn married the dau of the former king
1 CONC  of Norway, former king of Northumberland & grand dau of the kings of Denmark & Norway. Then again, Ragnhild had Arnfinn murdered, so I guess she didn’t think it was such a win-win
