0 @N6000000002187914742@ NOTE Eric I of Norway
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eric Bloodaxe (Old Norse:Eiríkr blóðöx, Icelandic:Eiríkur blóðöx, Norwegian:Eirik Blodøks) (circa 885 – 954), was the second king of Norway (930-934) and the eldest son of his father Harald Fairhair. Once the power was in his hand
1 CONC s, he began to quarrel with his other brothers and had four of them killed, including Bjørn Farmann and later Olaf and Sigrød. A Latin text describes him as fratris interfector (brother-slayer), and it is entirely conceivable that
1 CONC  the "blood" component of his cognomen refers to his propensity for fratricide.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 920, he undertook a Viking expedition to Bjarmaland, in northern Russia.
1 CONT
1 CONT His youngest brother, Haakon returned from England and won support from the Norwegian nobles to oust Eric in 934; Eric's rule was hard and despotic, and this would account for the alacrity with which the nobles joined forces to ou
1 CONC st him. After waging unsuccessful campaigns to regain the throne, Eric moved to the Orkney Islands and later to the Kingdom of Jorvik. He was initially met warmly by Athelstan, who made Eric ruler in Northumbria, with a brief to p
1 CONC rovide a defence against the ravages of the Scots, and the Irish. His rule in Northumbria soon degenerated, however, and he was expelled by the populace and betrayed by the earl of Bernicia, Osulf, to one Earl Maccus and killed in
1 CONC  battle at Stainmore, Westmorland, in 954.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Harald Fairhair King of Norway
1 CONT 930–934 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Haakon the Good
