0 @N6000000001783438992@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT Ragnvald Eysteinsson, Jarl of More and Romsdal, nicknamed The Wise and The Mighty, whose insignia was a wolf's head, campaigned with his second cousin, Harald, to unify Norway. They were assisted by the Earls of Lade. Lade is situ
1 CONC ated in the eastern part of Trondheim, bordering on Trondheimsfjiord.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the 9th. Century, a powerful group established themselves around Trondheimsfjord, called the Hlaoajarlar, Earls of Lade, after their lands situated in present-day Trondheim. These people originated in Halogaland, a vast strip o
1 CONC f northern territory stretching to the borders of Finland and Russia. Its name means land of the aurora. Settlement was sparse, and life revolved around hunting and fishing. The result of these activities, especially whaling, made
1 CONC  the men in control of Halogaland vastly rich. It was of vital interest to the warrior chieftains of Trondheim to protect their trade routes to Halogaland. The unification of Norway came about to protect these trade routes from pi
1 CONC rate-jarls.
1 CONT
1 CONT c. 868 Ragnvald fought on the side of his kinsman against these pirate-jarls, and was rewarded with the territories of More and Romsdal. This was after the Battle of Solskel, in which Jarl Hunthiof of More and Jarl Novke of Romsda
1 CONC l were defeated. More and Romsdal are in western Norway, bordering on the Atlantic Ocean.
1 CONT
1 CONT c. 875 Harald and Ragnvald also conquered lands in Shetland, Orkney, Hebrides, and the Isle of Man. On the journey back to Norway, Haraldr gave the Earldom of Shetland and Orkney to Ragnvald as recompense for the death of his son,
1 CONC  Ivar, during the campaign. [This is the traditional account, however there is every possibility that Ragnvald seized these territories independently of Harald.] Ragnvald gave these lands to his brother, Sigurd. Gaelic annals reco
1 CONC rding the wasting of Pictland in the reign of Domnall mac Custantin, 889-900, are probably referring to the activities of Sigurd and his ally, Thorstein the Red, son of Olaf Hvitr of Dublin. They made great incursions into Caithne
1 CONC ss and Sutherland. [W. F. Skene, Chronicles of the Picts, 1867.]
1 CONT
1 CONT c. 884 The unification of Norway was a gradual process, but the the Battle of Hafrsfjord has traditionally been regarded as the decisive battle. Most modern scholars agree that the Battle of Hafrsfjord took place during the 880s: 
1 CONC 'A great battle began, which was both hard and long; but at last King Haraldr gained the day. There King Eirik fell, and King Sulke, with his brother Earl Sote. Thor Haklang, who was a great berserk, had laid his ship against King
1 CONC  Harald's, and there was above all measure a desperate attack, until Thor Haklang fell, and his whole ship was cleared of men. Then King Kjotve fled to a little isle outside, on which there was a good place of strength. Thereafter
1 CONC  all his men fled, some to their ships, some up to the land; and the latter ran southwards over the country of Jadar.' [Harald Harfager's Saga, ch. 19.]
1 CONT
1 CONT c. 891 Ragnvald's son, Rolf Ragnvaldsson, was banished from Norway. He joined the war band of his uncle, Malahule Eysteinsson, who would not submit to Harald's rule, and who had been campaigning in France for a number of years. Th
1 CONC is war band ultimately wrested control of Normandy from France, Rolf becoming more widely known as Rolf The Ganger, 872-931, first Duke of Normandy. Rolf's crime was that 'One summer, as he was coming from the eastward on a viking
1 CONC 's expedition to the coast of Viken, he landed there and made a cattle foray. As King Harald happened, just at that time, to be in Viken, he heard of it, and was in a great rage; for he had forbid, by the greatest punishment, the 
1 CONC plundering within the bounds of the country. [Harald Harfager's Saga, ch. 24.]
1 CONT
1 CONT c. 892 Sigurd Eysteinsson defended Orkney against the Scottish Earl Maelbrigte - nicknamed Maelbrigte Tusk because of his protruding teeth - defeating him. He had the severed heads of the defeated strapped to his mens' saddles, hi
1 CONC s saddle bearing Maelbrigte's head. When Sigurd went to spur his horse, to commence what I suppose was a victory parade, his calf was pierced by a tooth sticking out of Maelbrigte's mouth! This proved fatal. Sigurd died of an infe
1 CONC ction. He was laid in a mound at Cyderhall - Sigurd's Howe - near Dornoch. Sigurd's son, Guthorm Sigurdsson, briefly succeeded his father by one winter, but died without issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT The manner of Guthorm's death is not recorded. However, the sagas portray this to be a time of much conflict, with sons of Harald Harfager being actively engaged in open hostilities against Ragnvald Eysteinsson's family, of whom t
1 CONC hey were jealous. It can be noted that Ragnvald had initially fought against his second cousin, only joining him when that seemed to be the sensible option. As a result of Harald's forced redistribution of land in Norway, Ragnvald
1 CONC 's family had become immensly rich and politically powerful. In this latter respect, note Sigurd Eysteinsson's alliance with the Vikings of Dublin. Ragnvald's family were now a serious threat to Harald, especially if allying thems
1 CONC elves with the Earls of Lade, who had not acknowledged Harald's hegemony. The death of Sigurd would have been a propitious time for Harald to place one of his sons as Jarl of Orkney, thus creating a loyal outpost to his kingdom. I
1 CONC t would seem entirely probable that Guthorm Sigurdsson was an early casualty of Harald's political intent.
1 CONT
1 CONT c. 893 When Ragnvald heard of Guthorm's death, he sent one of his sons, Hallad Ragnvaldsson, to be the third Jarl. He did not defend Orkney well against what the sagas portray as repeated Viking attacks, and returned to Norway in 
1 CONC disgrace. [Harald Harfager's Saga, ch. 22.]
1 CONT
1 CONT c. 894 Ragnvald, seeking to secure Orkney, summoned three of his sons to a war-counsel. It was decided that that his eldest son, Hrollager Ragnvaldsson, would campaign in Iceland; the one-eyed Turf-Einar Ragnvaldsson, 866-910, wou
1 CONC ld sail for Orkney, and Thorer The Silent Ragnvaldsson, 873-925, would remain in Norway. [Harald Harfager's Saga, ch. 27.] Turf-Einar was a redoutable warrior. The stakes had risen. Harald's sons, Halfdan Haaleg and Gudrod Ljome, 
1 CONC sons by Snaefrid Svasedottir, surrounded Ragnvald in his house one night, and burnt him and sixty of his men to death. Gudrod claimed Ragnvald's lands, and Halfdan sailed to Orkney to slay Turf-Einar. It would seem improbable that
1 CONC  such an outrage could have happened without Harald's permission.
1 CONT
1 CONT Turf-Einar was taken by surprise when attacked by Halfdan Haaleg, and fled to the mainland, but returned a short while after ready for battle. He defeated Halfdan in a sea battle, after which, 'Einar and his men lay all night with
1 CONC out tents, and when it was light in the morning they searched the whole island and killed every man they could lay hold of ....... Earl Einar went up to Halfdan, and cut a spread eagle upon his back, by striking his sword through 
1 CONC his back into his belly, dividing his ribs from the backbone down to his loins, and tearing out his lungs; and so Halfdan was killed ....... Then Earl Einar took possession of the Orkney Isles as before. Now when these tidings cam
1 CONC e to Norway, Halfdan's brothers took it much to heart, and thought that his death demanded vengeance; and many were of the same opinion.' [Harald Harfager's Saga, chs. 30-32.]
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald Harfager, being aware of the risks posed by a protracted conflict, did not share that opinion. He forced Gudrod to relinquish Ragnvald's lands, giving them to Ragnvald's son, Thorer, to whom he also gave his daughter, Alof,
1 CONC  in marriage. This marriage was by way of weregild - man price - a reparational payment. He went to Orkney with a great force - how could he not ? - but only levied a token fine of sixty gold marks in payment of Haldan's death. [H
1 CONC arald Harfager's Saga, ibid.] Before leaving Turf-Einar, to better fix an idea of him and his descendants in our minds, I would like to give an example of the old Norse language, as developed on Orkney, and similar to that spoken 
1 CONC in the high fells of North Yorkshire up to the 16th. Century; though I am not sure that these words or anything closely resembling them were ever said by Turf-Einar!
1 CONT
1 CONT * Thorer and Alof had issue: Jorund Thorersson, who settled in Iceland, and took land near Lake Udarvatu, living on a farm he called Grund. His son Mar Jorundsson settled a farm called Marstad. Jorund's uncle, Hrollager Ragnvaldss
1 CONC on, established his farm at Felzhverfi. [S. Laing, Journal of a Residence in Norway, p. 261, 1851.]
1 CONT <http://stanhopefamilyorigins.com/>
0 @N6000000003796257530@ NOTE URL: http://www.orkneyjar.com/history/historicalfigures/sigmighty.htm
1 CONT
1 CONT Earl Sigurd the Powerful  
1 CONT
1 CONT The first Earl of Orkney
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigurd Eysteinsson - or Earl Sigurd the Powerful - was the first Earl of Orkney.
1 CONT
1 CONT But although the Orkneyinga Saga makes it clear that Earl Sigurd I was one of the three great earls of Orkney, it contains very few details of his reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigurd enters the saga as the forecastleman of one of King Harald Fairhair’s ships on the voyage of conquest into Orkney, Shetland and the Western Isles. 
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the saga, the Norwegian king had sailed westwards to deal with Vikings who, after raiding Norway throughout the summer, were making the Northern Isles their base.
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald’s forces conquered Orkney and Shetland before going on to the Hebrides and the Isle of Man.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the voyage Sigurd’s brother, Earl Rognvald of More, received the Earldom of Orkney from King Harald as compensation for the loss of his son, Ivar.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigurd gains the Earldom
1 CONT
1 CONT Rognvald had no intentions of staying in the islands so passed the Earldom on to Sigurd, who became Earl Sigurd I of Orkney. As Earl, Sigurd ruled wisely and became very powerful - but unfortunately the saga says little more of hi
1 CONC s reign. Instead the reader is hurled into the tale of Earl Sigurd’s death - a story that remains firmly in the memories of Orcadians today as a folk origin for the Kirkwall Ba’ game.
1 CONT
1 CONT Earl Sigurd had formed an alliance with Thorstein the Red, travelling south into Scotland where they conquered all of Caithness and large parts of Argyll, Moray and Ross.
1 CONT
1 CONT Glossing over the exact details of the campaign, the saga goes on to tell us that Earl Sigurd constructed a stronghold in Moray before mentioning a feud between Sigurd and a local magnate Maelbrigte. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The reason for the two men’s enmity is not given but it was undoubtedly to do with the Orkney earl’s forays into Scottish territory. Whatever the cause, both men agreed they should meet to settle their differences, each taking no 
1 CONC more than forty men.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigurd, however, decided that the Scots were not to be trusted and turned up with eighty warriors - two warriors mounted on each of his forty horses.
1 CONT
1 CONT Orcadian Treachery
1 CONT
1 CONT Maelbrigte was aware that treachery was afoot when he noticed there were two feet on each side of every horse. Knowing he had been betrayed by the Orkney earl, he instructed his men to fight on and slay two of the enemy each. A ba
1 CONC ttle ensued and, despite their bravery, the outnumbered Scottish side perished and Maelbrigte was slain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Elated at his victory, Sigurd had the heads of his vanquished enemies severed and strapped to the saddles of his warriors as a show of his triumph.
1 CONT
1 CONT Snatching up his grizzly trophy, Sigurd followed suit and fastened Maelbrigte’s head to his saddle. After mounting his horse the earl’s forces headed north but Maelbrigte had his revenge. While spurring his horse during the ride h
1 CONC ome, Earl Sigurd’s leg was scratched by Maelbrigte’s protruding buck-tooth.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigurd's Howe
1 CONT
1 CONT The scratch became infected and before long Earl Sigurd the Powerful died. He was buried at “Ekkjalsbakki” - the banks of the River Oykell in Scotland. Although the exact location is unknown, the area of Earl Sigurd’s burial place
1 CONC  is now known as Ciderhall - a corruption of the Norse words meaning “Sigurd’s Howe”. See side panel for further details.
1 CONT
1 CONT His son, Guthrom, ruled the earldom for one winter before dying childless. Earl Rognvald of More son was then sent from Norway to become earl.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ======================================================================================================================
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT URL: http://www.orkneyjar.com/history/vikingorkney/torfeinar.htm
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Torf-Einar and the Blood Eagle
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT "After [Sigurd] Torf-Einar, a son of Ragnvald, took the earldom, and was long earl, and was a man of great power"
1 CONT Heimskringla - Saga of Olaf Haraldson
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of Earl Sigurd the Mighty around 893AD, the Orkney earldom passed to his son Guthorm.
1 CONT
1 CONT Guthorm died childless "after one winter", leaving the earldom with no direct heir, so Rognvald of Møre sent his son, Hallad, from Norway to Orkney. Repeated Viking raids after Hallad's arrival, and his inability to deal with them
1 CONC , prompted the new earl to give up the earldom and return to the safety of Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT Next in line was Rognvald's son Hrolf, who was later to found the Norman dynasty in France. Hrolf, however, was away warring in the Baltic so eventually the earldom passed to Rognvald's bastard son Einar.
1 CONT
1 CONT Einar Rognvaldsson's accession to the earldom pleased his father greatly. A bastard son, Einar was undoubtedly a thorn in his father's side.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Considering the kind of mother you have," said the Earl, "slave bornon each side of her family, you are not likely to make much of a ruler. The sooner you leave and the later you return, the happier I'll be."
1 CONT Orkneyinga Saga, Chapter 6
1 CONT
1 CONT A renowned poet, the saga decribes Einar - or Torf-Einar as he came to be known - as ugly and one-eyed. Despite his father's words, the "low born" son of a slave went on to be an able ruler and warrior.
1 CONT
1 CONT Danish vikings
1 CONT
1 CONT After Hallad's retreat to Norway, two Danish Vikings - Thorir Treebeard and Kalf Scurvy - had established a base in Orkney. Einar, equipped with a shipload of men travelled first to Shetland before heading to Orkney, where he defe
1 CONC ated the Danes in battle. He then "laid the lands under him, and made himself the greatest chief."
1 CONT
1 CONT The treachery of Halfdan
1 CONT
1 CONT Einar came into conflict with Norway, after Halfdan Longlegs, the son of the Norwegian King, Harald Fairhair, attacked Einar's father Rognvald. Halfdan, together with his brother Gudrod Gleam, were rebellious troublemakers who pre
1 CONC yed on Norway's earls.
1 CONT
1 CONT Attacking the aged Rognvald, Halfdan had the earl killed, burning him alive in his house, before fleeing to Orkney to escape the wrath of King Harald. In Orkney, Halfdan declared himself king, and Earl Einar fled to Scotland. Late
1 CONC r the same year, after gathering an army, Einar returned where he defeated Halfdan in a naval battle off North Ronaldsay. After the battle Halfdan leapt from his ship to escape Einar and swam to safety.
1 CONT
1 CONT At first light the following morning, Einar and his men scoured North Ronaldsay. Eventually, the earl, who was renowned for the keeness of sight of his one eye, spotted something moving by the shore. His men moved to intercept and
1 CONC  they captured a bedraggled Halfdan.
1 CONT
1 CONT Einar, seeking revenge for the death of his father, slaughtered Halfdan, the Orkneyinga Saga explaining with relish that Einar carved the notorious blóðörn - blood-eagle - on his struggling captive:
1 CONT
1 CONT "Einar had his ribs cut from the spine with a sword and the lungs pulled out through the slits in his back. He dedicated the victim to Odin as a victory offering."
1 CONT Orkneyinga Saga
1 CONT
1 CONT Though barbaric, Einar's revenge remains touching, for although Rognvald seems to have had no time for his bastard son, Einar's revenge at least hints that he held his father in some esteem.
1 CONT
1 CONT Earl Einar Rognvaldsson died peacefully in his bed, leaving three sons - Arnkel, Erlend and Thorfinn Skullsplitter.
0 @N6000000002187576820@ NOTE Ragnvald Eysteinsson
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT (Redirected from Ragnvald, Earl of Moer)
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Ragnvald "the Wise" Eysteinsson (830-890) (Old Norse: Rögnvaldr Mærajarl), Jarl of Sunnmøre, Nordmøre and Romsdal, was born in Maer, Nord-Trøndelag, Norway and died at the Orkney Islands.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was son of King Eystein "Glumra (the Noisy)" Ivarsson of Oppland. His maternal grandfather was King Ragnvald the Mountain-High of Vestfold. One of his paternal great-grandfathers was King Halfdan the Old of Oppland.
1 CONT
1 CONT His second wife was Ragnhild Hrolfsdottir (Raghldr (Hldr) Hrolfsdóttir), daughter of Hrolf Nefia (Hrolfr Nefjaa). Ragnvald was the father of Hrolf Ganger, the founder of Normandy, and Turf-Einar, ancestor of the jarls of Orkney.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is a direct ancestor of William I of England, Edward III of England, James I of England, and Elizabeth II of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland. He is therefore the ancestor of most of the royal families o
1 CONC f Europe.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT   This Norwegian biographical article is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
1 CONT
1 CONT   This biography of a European noble is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT burnt to death in his house. Earl of Maere, Raumdahl, Orkneys, and Shetlands. Source: Brian Tompsett, Dan Pomerleau, Leo van de Pas
0 @N6000000001744855012@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [Brøderbund WFT Vol. 2, Ed. 1, Tree #1241, Date of Import: May 8, 1997]
1 CONT
1 CONT !JARL OF MORE
1 CONT from Snorre Sturlason (lived 1179-1241), Heimskringla, or the Lives of the Norse Kings (NY: Dover, 1932, 1990):
1 CONT
1 CONT Möre is situated on the coast south of Trondheim. Was ruled by King Huntjov, and his son Salve Klove, both great warriors. They engaged in a battle with King Harald Hairfair near Solskel. Harald won the two shires of More and Raum
1 CONC sdale. "Ragnvald, Jarl of More, son of Eystein Glumra, then became King Harald's man. King Harald set him as chief over these two shires, Nordmore and Raumsdale, and gave him the support of both leaders and bonders and also ships 
1 CONC to keep the land from unrest. Ragnvald was called the Mighty, or the Shrewd, and it is said that both by-names were true." (p. 48)
1 CONT
1 CONT Later, Harald made Ragnvald Jarl over both shires. Sturlason gives an account of Ragnvald's conquest south to the Fjords, where he surrounded and burned the house of King Vemund and 90 men, seizing Vemund's ships and chattels... (
1 CONC pp. 50-58)
1 CONT
1 CONT In a battle on the Isle of Man fell Ivor, son of Ragnvald the Jarl of Möre, and "to requite his death King Harald gave the Jarl Ragnvald the Orkneys and Shetland Islands, but Ragnvald straightway gave both lands to his brother Sig
1 CONC urd," who then became a Jarl. To join Sigurd came Torstein the Red (son of Olav the White, King in Dublin until 870) and Aud the Pensive." In Scotland, they "possessed themselves of Caithness and Sutherland to the River Oykell. Si
1 CONC gurd the Jarl slew the Scottish Jarl, Melbridge Tooth, and bound his head to his saddle strap, but the calf of his leg was wounded by a tooth which stood out from it; he festered and he got his death from it; he was buried in a ho
1 CONC we by the river Oykell. Then his son Guthorm ruled over the land for a winter and died childless. After that, the Vikings, Danes and Norsemen settled firmly in these lands."
1 CONT
1 CONT "Ragnvald the Jarl was King Harald's dearest friend and the king valued him highly. Ragnvald had, for his wife, Hild the daughter of Rolf Nevja, and their sons were Rolf and Tore. He also had bastard sons, one called Hallad, the n
1 CONC ext Einar and the third Rollaug. They were full grown when their legitimate brothers were still children.
1 CONT
1 CONT When King Harald Hairfair was forty years old, many of his sons were fully grown and they all ripened early to manhood. And it came about that they little liked the fact that the king gave them no kingdoms, but set jarls in every 
1 CONC shire; they thought jarls were not so high-born as themselves. One spring Halvdan and Gudrod went off with a great band of men; they came suddenly upon Ragnvald, Jarl of More, drew a ring around the house and burned him in it with
1 CONC  sixty men....Gudrod set himself firmly where Ragnvald the Jarl had formerly had his seat. When King Harald learned that, he straightaway went against Gudrod with a great army and Gudrod saw no lot for himself but to put himself i
1 CONC n King Harald's power; the king sent him east to Agder. King Harald then set Tore, the son of Ragnvald, over More and married him to his own daughter Alov, who was called Arbot. Jarl Tore the silent then had such rule as his fathe
1 CONC r Ragnvald had had."
1 CONT
1 CONT quoted from Snorre Sturlason (c. 1179-1241), Heimskringla, or the Lives of the Norse Kings (NY: Dover, 1932, 1990) pp. 59-64
1 CONT
1 CONT A brief history of the Medieval Kings of Norway:
1 CONT
1 CONT Norse King Halvdan the Black (b. 820; ruled 839-60). During this time Turgesius or Torgils subdued Irish by conquering Dublin. Then son Harald Hairfair (b. 850; ruled 860-933) who united Norway into one kingdom, and outlawed Rolf 
1 CONC (the son of his closest friend) who eventually went to conquer Normandy. Rolf the Ganger's (called Rollon by the French) conquest of North France (Normandy) is legendary; his half-brother Einar became Earl of Orkney, from whom man
1 CONC y subsequent Earls of Orkney have descended.
1 CONT
1 CONT -King Hacon the Good was the foster son of Hacon Athelstan (King of England, grandson of King Alfred)--H the G left England for Norway on the death of his father in 933. Then followed Kings Hacon the Jarl, Olav Trygvason, Olav Har
1 CONC aldson (St. Olav) who d. 1030, and Magnus the Good (son of Olav). Made treaty with King Hardicanute (Canute or Knut) to succeed to thrones of Denmark and England--claimed Crown from Edward the Confessor of England, but allowed him
1 CONC  to still rule.
1 CONT
1 CONT -His uncle, King Harald Hardrade, consented at request of Earl Tosti (brother of Harold Godwinson, last of the Old English kings) to bring an army to England. Defeated English army outside of York then went to battle against Godwi
1 CONC nson--Norsemen were defeated in a fierce battle and Tosti and Harald were slain. William the Conqueror (descendant of Rolf the Ganger) arrived 4 days later and killed King Harold Godwinson to gain the crown of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT -Harald's son Olav Kyrre the Peaceful followed as King, then son Magnus Barefoot, who ruled the same time as Henry I (son of William the Conqueror) of Eng, and led expeditions to attack England from West and Ireland. Killed in Ire
1 CONC land in 1103.
1 CONT
1 CONT -Then Sigurd the Crusaderwas King, who was Earl of Orkney (son of Magnus Barefoot). Voyage to Jerusalem and Constantinople. d. 1130.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Viking Age was ending. Norse parted with last 2 British Isles: Orkney and Shetland--in 1468.
1 CONT
1 CONT Final King of this period: Magnus Erlingson (1161-77)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Genealogical Source: Church of JC of the LDS "Ancestral File" CD-Rom database, ver 4.17.[Brøderbund WFT Vol. 2, Ed. 1, Tree #1241, Date of Import: May 8, 1997]
1 CONT
1 CONT !JARL OF MORE
1 CONT from Snorre Sturlason (lived 1179-1241), Heimskringla, or the Lives of the Norse Kings (NY: Dover, 1932, 1990):
1 CONT
1 CONT Möre is situated on the coast south of Trondheim. Was ruled by King Huntjov, and his son Salve Klove, both great warriors. They engaged in a battle with King Harald Hairfair near Solskel. Harald won the two shires of More and Raum
1 CONC sdale. "Ragnvald, Jarl of More, son of Eystein Glumra, then became King Harald's man. King Harald set him as chief over these two shires, Nordmore and Raumsdale, and gave him the support of both leaders and bonders and also ships 
1 CONC to keep the land from unrest. Ragnvald was called the Mighty, or the Shrewd, and it is said that both by-names were true." (p. 48)
1 CONT
1 CONT Later, Harald made Ragnvald Jarl over both shires. Sturlason gives an account of Ragnvald's conquest south to the Fjords, where he surrounded and burned the house of King Vemund and 90 men, seizing Vemund's ships and chattels... (
1 CONC pp. 50-58)
1 CONT
1 CONT In a battle on the Isle of Man fell Ivor, son of Ragnvald the Jarl of Möre, and "to requite his death King Harald gave the Jarl Ragnvald the Orkneys and Shetland Islands, but Ragnvald straightway gave both lands to his brother Sig
1 CONC urd," who then became a Jarl. To join Sigurd came Torstein the Red (son of Olav the White, King in Dublin until 870) and Aud the Pensive." In Scotland, they "possessed themselves of Caithness and Sutherland to the River Oykell. Si
1 CONC gurd the Jarl slew the Scottish Jarl, Melbridge Tooth, and bound his head to his saddle strap, but the calf of his leg was wounded by a tooth which stood out from it; he festered and he got his death from it; he was buried in a ho
1 CONC we by the river Oykell. Then his son Guthorm ruled over the land for a winter and died childless. After that, the Vikings, Danes and Norsemen settled firmly in these lands."
1 CONT
1 CONT "Ragnvald the Jarl was King Harald's dearest friend and the king valued him highly. Ragnvald had, for his wife, Hild the daughter of Rolf Nevja, and their sons were Rolf and Tore. He also had bastard sons, one called Hallad, the n
1 CONC ext Einar and the third Rollaug. They were full grown when their legitimate brothers were still children.
1 CONT
1 CONT When King Harald Hairfair was forty years old, many of his sons were fully grown and they all ripened early to manhood. And it came about that they little liked the fact that the king gave them no kingdoms, but set jarls in every 
1 CONC shire; they thought jarls were not so high-born as themselves. One spring Halvdan and Gudrod went off with a great band of men; they came suddenly upon Ragnvald, Jarl of More, drew a ring around the house and burned him in it with
1 CONC  sixty men....Gudrod set himself firmly where Ragnvald the Jarl had formerly had his seat. When King Harald learned that, he straightaway went against Gudrod with a great army and Gudrod saw no lot for himself but to put himself i
1 CONC n King Harald's power; the king sent him east to Agder. King Harald then set Tore, the son of Ragnvald, over More and married him to his own daughter Alov, who was called Arbot. Jarl Tore the silent then had such rule as his fathe
1 CONC r Ragnvald had had."
1 CONT
1 CONT quoted from Snorre Sturlason (c. 1179-1241), Heimskringla, or the Lives of the Norse Kings (NY: Dover, 1932, 1990) pp. 59-64
1 CONT
1 CONT A brief history of the Medieval Kings of Norway:
1 CONT
1 CONT Norse King Halvdan the Black (b. 820; ruled 839-60). During this time Turgesius or Torgils subdued Irish by conquering Dublin. Then son Harald Hairfair (b. 850; ruled 860-933) who united Norway into one kingdom, and outlawed Rolf 
1 CONC (the son of his closest friend) who eventually went to conquer Normandy. Rolf the Ganger's (called Rollon by the French) conquest of North France (Normandy) is legendary; his half-brother Einar became Earl of Orkney, from whom man
1 CONC y subsequent Earls of Orkney have descended.
1 CONT
1 CONT -King Hacon the Good was the foster son of Hacon Athelstan (King of England, grandson of King Alfred)--H the G left England for Norway on the death of his father in 933. Then followed Kings Hacon the Jarl, Olav Trygvason, Olav Har
1 CONC aldson (St. Olav) who d. 1030, and Magnus the Good (son of Olav). Made treaty with King Hardicanute (Canute or Knut) to succeed to thrones of Denmark and England--claimed Crown from Edward the Confessor of England, but allowed him
1 CONC  to still rule.
1 CONT
1 CONT -His uncle, King Harald Hardrade, consented at request of Earl Tosti (brother of Harold Godwinson, last of the Old English kings) to bring an army to England. Defeated English army outside of York then went to battle against Godwi
1 CONC nson--Norsemen were defeated in a fierce battle and Tosti and Harald were slain. William the Conqueror (descendant of Rolf the Ganger) arrived 4 days later and killed King Harold Godwinson to gain the crown of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT -Harald's son Olav Kyrre the Peaceful followed as King, then son Magnus Barefoot, who ruled the same time as Henry I (son of William the Conqueror) of Eng, and led expeditions to attack England from West and Ireland. Killed in Ire
1 CONC land in 1103.
1 CONT
1 CONT -Then Sigurd the Crusaderwas King, who was Earl of Orkney (son of Magnus Barefoot). Voyage to Jerusalem and Constantinople. d. 1130.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Viking Age was ending. Norse parted with last 2 British Isles: Orkney and Shetland--in 1468.
1 CONT
1 CONT Final King of this period: Magnus Erlingson (1161-77)
